                <pb facs="1285" n="1244" sameAs="frus:frus1949v03/pg1244"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>At its 43rd meeting, July 7, 1949, the National Security Council considered the
                    question of the future course of United States action with respect to Austria.
                    Secretary Acheson agreed that a further
                    study should be made of the problem of training an Austrian Army to determine
                    what was necessary from the standpoint of United States security interests. The
                    military representative, General McNarney, felt that unless
                    adequate Austrian forces were trained, equipped, and in operation <pb
                        facs="1286" n="1245" xml:id="pg1245"/> before the withdrawal of occupation
                    forces, the danger of Communist revolution would be serious, since revolutionary
                    organizations would have been created in the Soviet zone prior to that time. For
                    this reason the National Military Establishment considered that the existing
                    plan, to train one <hi rendition="#italic">gendarmerie</hi> regiment, was not
                    sufficient. (Executive Secretariat files)</p>
            </div>
